Chapter: DevState
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The debugger's own state: what it is watching, and what the last
 expression came to.

 `docs/Designs/Active/Tools/DEVELOPER-DEBUGGER.md` names this and
 `ExprEval` as the two Phase-1 modules that never landed, and says watches
 have nowhere to live without it. This is that place.

 WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CARRY, AND WHY THAT IS THREE THINGS.

 `macros`, `serial-mirror` and `serial-remote` are in the design's record.
 This chapter used to say they would move in here once their modules
 existed. Both modules landed on 2026-07-28 and neither field did, and
 that is a decision rather than an omission.

 `Macro.codex` owns its own `MacroTable`, because a macro table is data
 with its own bounds, refusals and splitter, and a chapter that owns them
 can be tested as a unit -- which is the same argument that put the watch
 table here rather than in `MemInspector`. The two serial flags stayed in
 the console's own record because they are not debugger DATA at all: they
 govern how the console does I/O, and nothing pure has an opinion about
 them.

 So the design's single unified record is now three chapters, each holding
 what it can test. This chapter is the watch table, and that is still the
 whole of it.

 `last-result` is in the design's record too, and it is left out for a
 different and stronger reason: **the console already has it.** The
 debugger's `last-address` is what an evaluated expression sets and what
 `$` reads, and it has been that since ExprEval landed. Adding a second
 field holding the same number would make two places that must agree and
 nothing that checks they do -- which is the shape of the three direction
 constants, the three partition GUIDs, and every other duplicated fact
 this project has had to go back and derive instead. One value, one home.

 So this chapter is the watch table, and that is the whole of it.

 THE WHOLE THING IS PURE. `peek-byte` carries no effect row, so taking a
 snapshot and comparing one are ordinary functions and this chapter can be
 tested without a machine. That is not an accident of the builtin: it is
 why the watch logic lives here rather than in the console, which cannot
 be tested without one.

 PURE INCLUDES NOT MUTATING THE STATE HANDED IN, and the first cut of this
 chapter failed that while claiming it. Every state transition built its
 result with `__record-set st "ds-watches" ...`, which stores into the
 field and returns THE SAME record, so a caller that kept its own `st`
 found the new watch in it. Two CDX6020 warnings said so, at the two
 constructors.

 It is a fresh `DevState` at all three sites now. The record has exactly
 one field, so constructing one costs less than the argument for keeping
 the mutation would, and the purity claim above is true rather than
 aspirational.

 Nothing in the console depended on the aliasing -- `debug-watch-here` and
 `debug-watch-check` both thread the returned state forward, which is the
 single-owner discipline that made the mutation survivable in the first
 place. What it was costing was the ability to reason about this chapter
 without knowing that.

 We say:

Section: Bounds

 Both caps exist because there is no collector under this. Thirty-two is
 the design's figure. The length cap is mine and is about TIME rather than
 space -- a snapshot is a checksum, so its storage does not grow with the
 region, but every check re-reads every byte, and a watch over a gigabyte
 would hang the console with no way back.

 A refusal says which cap was hit. Silently dropping the thirty-third
 watch would leave an operator watching something they believe they are
 not.

  dev-max-watches : Integer = 32
  dev-max-watch-len : Integer = 65536

Section: The Snapshot

 FNV-1a, 32-bit. It answers "did these bytes change", which is what the
 design asks of a watch, and it answers it in constant storage however
 long the region is.

 IT CANNOT SAY WHAT CHANGED, and that is a real limit rather than an
 oversight. Storing the bytes would allow an old-against-new diff and cost
 the region's length per watch; a checksum costs one integer. The dump is
 one command away (`dbg mem <addr>`), so the pairing is: this tells you
 THAT it moved, and MemInspector shows you what it is now. If a future
 reader wants the diff, the honest change is storing bytes under a small
 per-watch cap, not widening this.

 The 64-bit basis and prime do not fit a signed 64-bit Integer, which is
 why this is the 32-bit variant and every step masks back to 32 bits.

  fnv-offset-32 : Integer = 2166136261
  fnv-prime-32 : Integer = 16777619
  fnv-mask-32 : Integer = 4294967295

  dev-region-sum : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dev-region-sum (addr) (len) = dev-region-sum-loop addr len 0 fnv-offset-32

  dev-region-sum-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dev-region-sum-loop (addr) (len) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let x = bit-xor acc (peek-byte addr i)
   in dev-region-sum-loop addr len (i + 1) (bit-and (x * fnv-prime-32) fnv-mask-32)

Section: The State

  WatchEntry = record {
   we-label : Text,
   we-addr : Integer,
   we-len : Integer,
   we-sum : Integer
  }

  DevState = record {
   ds-watches : List WatchEntry
  }

  dev-state-new : DevState
  dev-state-new = DevState { ds-watches = [] }

  dev-watch-count : DevState -> Integer
  dev-watch-count (st) = list-length (st.ds-watches)

Section: Adding A Watch

 The result carries a message either way, because every path here has
 something the operator needs: which watch was added, or which cap
 refused it.

  WatchAdd = record {
   wa-state : DevState,
   wa-ok : Boolean,
   wa-message : Text
  }

  dev-watch-add : DevState, Text, Integer, Integer -> WatchAdd
  dev-watch-add (st) (label) (addr) (len) =
   if len <= 0 then WatchAdd { wa-state = st, wa-ok = False, wa-message = "watch length must be positive" }
   else if len > dev-max-watch-len then
     WatchAdd { wa-state = st, wa-ok = False, wa-message = "watch length " & show len & " exceeds " & show dev-max-watch-len }
   else if dev-watch-count st >= dev-max-watches then
     WatchAdd { wa-state = st, wa-ok = False, wa-message = "watch table full (" & show dev-max-watches & ")" }
   else let e = WatchEntry {
     we-label = label,
     we-addr = addr,
     we-len = len,
     we-sum = dev-region-sum addr len
   }
   in WatchAdd {
     wa-state = DevState { ds-watches = list-snoc (st.ds-watches) e },
     wa-ok = True,
     wa-message = "watching " & label & " at " & show addr & " for " & show len & " bytes"
   }

  dev-watch-clear : DevState -> DevState
  dev-watch-clear (st) = DevState { ds-watches = [] }

Section: Checking

 A check RE-SNAPSHOTS every watch it reports. Without that, one change
 makes a watch report as changed on every check for the rest of the
 session, and an operator watching two regions cannot tell which one moved
 this time. Re-arming means each report answers "since the last check",
 which is the question being asked.

 The report names the unchanged watches too. A checker that prints only
 what moved is silent when nothing does, and silence reads the same as a
 watch list that was never armed.

  WatchCheck = record {
   wc-state : DevState,
   wc-changed : Integer,
   wc-report : Text
  }

  dev-watch-check : DevState -> WatchCheck
  dev-watch-check (st) =
   let n = dev-watch-count st
   in if n == 0 then WatchCheck { wc-state = st, wc-changed = 0, wc-report = "no watches" }
   else dev-check-loop st 0 n [] 0 ""

  dev-check-loop : DevState, Integer, Integer, List WatchEntry, Integer, Text -> WatchCheck
  dev-check-loop (st) (i) (n) (acc) (changed) (report) =
   if i >= n then WatchCheck {
     wc-state = DevState { ds-watches = acc },
     wc-changed = changed,
     wc-report = report
   }
   else let e = list-at (st.ds-watches) i
   in let now = dev-region-sum (e.we-addr) (e.we-len)
   in let moved = now /= (e.we-sum)
   in let line = (e.we-label) & (if moved then " CHANGED" else " same")
   in let re-armed = WatchEntry {
        we-label = e.we-label,
        we-addr = e.we-addr,
        we-len = e.we-len,
        we-sum = now
      }
   in let next-changed = if moved then changed + 1 else changed
   in let next-report = if text-length report == 0 then line else report & ", " & line
   in dev-check-loop st (i + 1) n (list-snoc acc re-armed) next-changed next-report
